-\subsection{Initial checkpoints and paired contrasts}
 \label{app:initial-provenance}
-
-A restarted attempt can evaluate the initial model again and append
-another step-0 block. The initial-checkpoint rule considers file order,
-step numbers, draw indices, and subsequent training evaluations. Files
-without a later training-step evaluation are excluded. Within a file,
-a step-0 block is continued when the next evaluation at a different step
-has a positive step number. Exactly one continued block must exist across
-the specified source files, and it must contain the four draw indices
-$0,1,2,3$. Metric values and effect estimates do not enter this selection.
-The rule identifies usable initial evaluations for 27 of 36 cells with
-conflicting step-0 records; nine remain ambiguous.
-
-Initial-to-terminal contrasts pair each method with its own initial
-evaluation. Terminal replay effects compare two trained checkpoints and
-require no initial evaluation.
-
-The initial-block sensitivity analysis contains nine model--domain--method
-contrasts. Excluding all conflicting initial blocks gives one to four
-defined seeds per contrast; the unique-block rule gives five in each.
-For example, Falcon3-1B MaxRL has three defined seeds in Graph and one
-in PantryPlan under the exclusion rule, and five under the unique-block
-rule. The mean signs agree under both rules; nominal intervals are shown
-only for the complete five-seed contrasts. At Falcon3-1B, MaxRL and
-Re:Max increase conditional diversity on Graph, with intervals excluding
-zero, while MaxRL concentrates on Countdown, MathIR, and PantryPlan.
-This heterogeneous result does not support extending the concentration
